{"reference":"Romans 1","book":"Romans","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Romans 1:16-17 functions as a major statement of the letter’s gospel: it is presented as God’s power for salvation, reaching “Jew first” and also Greek, and it is linked to Habakkuk’s language about the righteous living by faith. The precise sense of “from faith unto faith,” as well as the speaker in the Habakkuk quotation, remains debated.","verse_refs":["Romans 1:1","Romans 1:16-17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_001_525f345b489808e1147b690c"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Called for the Gospel","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Paul introduces himself as called and set apart for God’s good news, with a mission directed toward “all the nations.” This identifies the vocation and scope claimed in the opening; it does not grant a right to compel belief or erase peoples’ cultures.","verse_refs":["Romans 1:1-6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_001_1c2544bf1008ca8ca7172ce6"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"Thanksgiving and Mutuality","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Paul’s opening thanks for the Roman believers and his continual prayerful desire to visit them should not be read as flattery, institutional branding, or proof that every reported feature is approved. His hoped-for visit is also not one-way: after speaking of giving a spiritual gift, he clarifies that both he and the Romans may be encouraged by each other’s faith.","verse_refs":["Romans 1:8","Romans 1:9-10","Romans 1:11-12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_001_9922a67a1b250b2dbe132a0f","reader_path_romans_001_768f2298d67dc2bcb3f11e52"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Key Interpretive Questions","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"“The righteousness of God” in verse 17 has been understood in several overlapping ways: God’s saving action, covenant faithfulness, rectitude, or a status-giving verdict. No single English gloss settles the phrase. The quotation belongs to Greek textual traditions that also receive Habakkuk elsewhere in the New Testament for distinct rhetorical purposes.","verse_refs":["Romans 1:17","Romans 1:18-32","Romans 1:26-27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_001_ec32f72faa76ea1fe0c358c1","reader_path_romans_001_9cc3265545a48af735c27cdf"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_5","text":"Verses 19-23 describe divine disclosure through created things, but they do not by themselves settle a later system of natural theology, the precise amount every person knows, or how responsibility relates to access and capacity. Those further questions go beyond what these verses specify.","verse_refs":["Romans 1:19-23","Romans 1:8-17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_001_9c4b1770d35aae6bd23dc84b"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_6","text":"The argument in 1:18-32 moves through a sequence of exchanges—honor for God exchanged for images, truth exchanged for a lie, and a resulting “giving up”—before reaching its wide vice list. Its language about passions, gender, nature, and acts uses ancient categories rather than modern categories of sexual orientation, identity, or same-sex marriage. The passage should therefore be read as part of its larger argument, not detached as a catalog for stigmatizing an outside group.","verse_refs":["Romans 1:18-32","Romans 1:24-27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_001_a72bb2954c7c1afb7e28e5ec"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading into Romans 2","blocks":[{"id":"block_7","text":"The argument immediately turns toward the person who judges others in Romans 2:1-5. That turn prevents the wrath language of Romans 1 from becoming permission for self-righteous targeting; the chapter’s indictment is not a secure position from which to condemn others.","verse_refs":["Romans 2:1-5"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_001_ca2c76153325ba1d10945567"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}